7-8061-5717-8325-c1d567664c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-42377" :question/problem "$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $L,M,$ and $N?$\n\n- The product $ML$ is well defined\n- The product $MN$ is well defined\n- The product $LN$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $L$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $M$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $N$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $M$ and $2$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $M$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $L,$ the matrices $M$ and $L$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{M}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{L}_{2\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $M$ and $3$ rows in $N.$ Since the number of columns in $M$ equals the number of rows in $N,$ the matrices $M$ and $N$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{M}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{N}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $L$ and $3$ rows in $N.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ equals the number of rows in $N,$ the matrices $L$ and $N$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{N}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-42377/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-42377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-42381/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bf2b8852-61f7-5898-9