d{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $O,P,$ and $R?$\n\n- The product $OP$ is well defined\n- The product $OR$ is well defined\n- The product $PR$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $O$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $P$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $O$ and $2$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $O$ equals the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $O$ and $P$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{O}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{✓}{P}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $O$ and $1$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $O$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $O$ and $R$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{O}_{3\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{R}_{1\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $P$ and $1$ rows in $R.$ Since the number of columns in $P$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $R,$ the matrices $P$ and $R$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{P}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{R}_{1\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statements II and III are false." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-42381/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-42381"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-22062/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "14fae76c-65d3-5849-9d5f-2fbd49f52056" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22062/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f7